      'When you have a pointer to a struct, `(*p).x` is awkward, so C provides the **arrow operator**: `p->x` means exactly the same thing.\n\nPassing a pointer avoids copying the whole struct and lets the function modify the original \u2014 the same reasoning as with any other type.\n\nThe convention is clear: `const Thing *` for read-only access, `Thing *` when the function needs to modify. Passing by value is fine for small structs like a point.',
