    title: 'The allocation functions',
    subtitle: 'malloc, calloc, realloc, free',
    concept:
      'Four functions from `<stdlib.h>` manage the heap.\n\n`malloc(bytes)` reserves uninitialised memory \u2014 the contents are garbage. `calloc(count, size)` reserves and **zeroes** it, which is safer when you will read before writing.\n\n`realloc(ptr, newBytes)` resizes an existing block, possibly moving it, and returns the new address. Always assign the result to a temporary first: if realloc fails it returns NULL, and overwriting your only pointer would leak the original block.\n\n`free(ptr)` releases it. Setting the pointer to NULL afterwards prevents accidental reuse.',
    example: '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int *zeroed = calloc(3, sizeof(int));\n    if (zeroed == NULL) return 1;\n    printf("%d %d %d\\n", zeroed[0], zeroed[1], zeroed[2]);\n\n    int *bigger = realloc(zeroed, 5 * sizeof(int));\n    if (bigger == NULL) {\n        free(zeroed);\n        return 1;\n    }\n    zeroed = bigger;\n\n    zeroed[3] = 40;\n    zeroed[4] = 50;\n    printf("%d %d\\n", zeroed[3], zeroed[4]);\n\n    free(zeroed);\n    zeroed = NULL;\n    printf("done\\n");\n    return 0;\n}',
    note: 'calloc gave three zeros; malloc would have given whatever was left in that memory.',
    task: 'Use calloc for 4 ints and print them (all zero). Then realloc to 6 ints, set the last two to 100 and 200, print all six, and free.',
    solution: H('    int *data = calloc(4, sizeof(int));\n    if (data == NULL) return 1;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) printf("%d ", data[i]);\n    printf("\\n");\n\n    int *bigger = realloc(data, 6 * sizeof(int));\n    if (bigger == NULL) {\n        free(data);\n        return 1;\n    }\n    data = bigger;\n\n    data[4] = 100;\n    data[5] = 200;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) printf("%d ", data[i]);\n    printf("\\n");\n\n    free(data);'),
    hint: 'Assign the realloc result to a temporary before overwriting your pointer.',
