    concept:
      'A heap string is a char buffer you allocate yourself. The size must be `strlen + 1` to leave room for the terminator \u2014 forgetting the `+ 1` is the most common buffer bug in C.\n\nThe standard idiom:\n\n```\nchar *copy = malloc(strlen(src) + 1);\nstrcpy(copy, src);\n```\n\nThis is what `strdup` does, and it lets a function return a string that outlives it \u2014 impossible with a local array. The caller then owns the memory and must free it.',
    example: '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nchar *duplicate(const char *src) {\n    char *copy = malloc(strlen(src) + 1);\n    if (copy == NULL) return NULL;\n    strcpy(copy, src);\n    return copy;\n}\n\nchar *join(const char *a, const char *b) {\n    char *out = malloc(strlen(a) + strlen(b) + 1);\n    if (out == NULL) return NULL;\n    strcpy(out, a);\n    strcat(out, b);\n    return out;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    char *copy = duplicate("heap string");\n    printf("%s\\n", copy);\n    printf("%zu\\n", strlen(copy));\n    free(copy);\n\n    char *both = join("hello, ", "world");\n    printf("%s\\n", both);\n    free(both);\n    return 0;\n}',
    note: 'join allocates the exact total plus one byte for the single terminator the result needs.',
    task: 'Write `char *shout(const char *src)` that returns a heap copy converted to uppercase. Apply it to `"quiet words"`, print the result and its length, and free it.',
    solution: '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nchar *shout(const char *src) {\n    size_t len = strlen(src);\n    char *out = malloc(len + 1);\n    if (out == NULL) return NULL;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n        out[i] = toupper((unsigned char)src[i]);\n    }\n    out[len] = \'\\0\';\n    return out;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    char *loud = shout("quiet words");\n    if (loud == NULL) return 1;\n    printf("%s\\n", loud);\n    printf("%zu\\n", strlen(loud));\n    free(loud);\n    return 0;\n}\n',
    hint: 'Allocate len + 1 bytes and write the terminator yourself.',
